{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll verify the four prior fixes against the new checkout, then review the full carry path for remaining gaps. I’ll use the identifiers in the generated “Previous round” section and keep the repository unchanged.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md; git diff 8300e10..43764959 -- pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'pwd; git status --short; cat .agents/skills/pose-review/SKILL.md; git diff 8300e10..43764959 -- pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go'","aggregated_output":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1106239546/worktree\n---\nname: pose-review\ndescription: Use for POSE pull-request or code review to verify controlled scope, preserved contracts, security and observability impact, risk-proportional validation, and escalation where applicable. Trigger keywords - review, code review, PR review, review opinion, ultrareview.\nwhen_to_use: Evaluating your own or another author's diff or PR under POSE. Use before commenting or approving to select rules, inspect validation evidence and prior decisions, and issue an actionable decision.\npose_schema_range: \"1-1\"\nclients: agents-skills, mcp, claude-code\ncapabilities: read\n---\n\n# Skill: pose-review\n\n## Required reading\n\n1. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).\n2. [`.pose/workflows/review.md`](../../../.pose/workflows/review.md).\n3. Applicable domain rules; security takes precedence in conflicts.\n\nResolve the selected task with `pose context --task <typed-or-qualified-artifact-ref> --json` before reviewing. Follow its canonical authority and stop on ambiguity, stale binding, unsupported metadata or `transfer-in-progress`. Use the qualified `xref:` scope for cross-project review; every cross-project write requires an explicit `POSE_PROJECT_ROOTS` binding and the current `context_revision` as `--expect-context`. Keep review evidence and its `POSE-Spec:` trailer in the authority repository.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Classify the change as feature, bugfix, refactor, documentation, or mixed.\n2. Run `pose assess tech-debt` during code review to ensure markers (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `stub`, `panic`) are covered by follow-ups or specs. Run `pose assess integrate` when touching inter-module contracts (Protobuf, Kafka, REST, MCP).\n3. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` to inspect effective criteria, components and tool requirements.\n4. Run active required tools first, record why each recommended tool was used or skipped, and keep completion tools deferred until attestation.\n5. Select rules with `pose suggest review --path <affected-dir>` for every mapped component.\n6. Search `.pose/knowledge/` for prior module decisions, accepted risks, and pending follow-ups.\n7. Require deterministic `pose validate --strict` evidence matching delivery targets.\n8. Evaluate every required plan criterion, including cross-component boundaries. When the change adds or alters a governance gate, also answer: what is the cheapest way to satisfy this gate in form without producing its value, and what stops that? Point to the adversarial corpus case that proves it (`pose-mcp/internal/cli/testdata/adversarial/README.md`), or record the gap.\n9. Classify findings as critical, high, medium, or low with evidence and expected action.\n10. Run `pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 14`; use recurrence escalation for a matching systemic signal.\n11. Create a handoff with `pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>` for accepted residual risk, monitoring, or deferred action.\n12. Seal the review subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`.\n13. Prepare the mechanical half with `pose review auto-attest <bundle-id> --reviewer agent:<id>` — without `--apply` — and read `review_attestation.pending`: those are the criteria no registered check answers, and they are yours to answer. Then record the attestation with `pose review attest`, giving every judgment criterion a conclusion. `--apply` on auto-attest records only when nothing is pending; it refuses otherwise, and never answers a judgment criterion for you. An explicit attestation records `--criterion ID|passed|evidence` (or `not-applicable` with a rationale, or `finding` naming a recorded finding) and `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]`; every finding needs a severity and an action, and an `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` finding also needs an owner, a rationale and a review date. A `passed` criterion may cite only evidence the sealed bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts. For a cross-project attestation, use the qualified `xref:` as the target argument to `pose review attest`, include `--expect-context <digest>` with `--apply`, and keep the explicit project binding; a bundle ID alone does not select an authority project.\n14. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; close only when both confirm a valid, approved attestation.\n15. Decide: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n16. When Contributor Mode is active, if review uncovers POSE linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal with `pose contribute stage --type enhancement --title \"<summary>\"`.\n\n## Output requirements\n\n- Completed Rules applied during review section.\n- Bundle/plan digests and dispositions for required, recommended and deferred completion tools.\n- Severity-classified findings with expected actions.\n- Clear and actionable final decision.\n- Handoff when residual risk is accepted.\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\nindex 9356cb01..d93bc0f6 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\n@@ -59,6 +59,7 @@ func (p carryPlan) carriedResult(id string) checkResult {\n // selection means every check runs; the record says why.\n func planCarry(root, resultsPath, mode, matrixSHA string, matrix validationMatrix, modules []validationModule) carryPlan {\n \tplan := carryPlan{record: &carryRecord{}}\n+\tengine, engineVerifiable := engineIdentityFrom(debug.ReadBuildInfo())\n \tunused := func(format string, args ...any) carryPlan {\n \t\tplan.record.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(format, args...)\n \t\treturn plan\n@@ -78,7 +79,9 @@ func planCarry(root, resultsPath, mode, matrixSHA string, matrix validationMatri\n \t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result ran in mode %q, this run is %q\", base.Mode, mode)\n \tcase base.MatrixSHA256 != matrixSHA:\n \t\treturn unused(\"the validation matrix changed since the canonical result\")\n-\tcase base.EngineVersion == \"\" || base.EngineVersion != validationEngineIdentity():\n+\tcase !engineVerifiable:\n+\t\treturn unused(\"this engine build was modified from its recorded revision, so its identity cannot be compared\")\n+\tcase base.EngineVersion == \"\" || base.EngineVersion != engine || strings.HasSuffix(base.EngineVersion, \".dirty\"):\n \t\treturn unused(\"the engine version differs from the canonical result's or was not recorded\")\n \tcase base.GitHead == \"\" || !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(base.GitHead):\n \t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records no usable head\")\n@@ -141,7 +144,7 @@ func planCarry(root, resultsPath, mode, matrixSHA string, matrix validationMatri\n // submodule or directory in a class stays a change.\n func carryExclusions(root, base, resultsPath string) []string {\n \texclude := []string{resultsPath}\n-\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", base, \"HEAD\", \"--\").Output()\n+\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", \"--no-renames\", base, \"HEAD\", \"--\").Output()\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\treturn exclude\n \t}\n@@ -167,10 +170,17 @@ func carryExclusions(root, base, resultsPath string) []string {\n // when the build tree was modified. Development builds share a version string,\n // so the revision is what tells two of them apart (R2).\n func validationEngineIdentity() string {\n+\tidentity, _ := engineIdentityFrom(debug.ReadBuildInfo())\n+\treturn identity\n+}\n+\n+// engineIdentityFrom builds the identity from build information. A build of a\n+// modified tree is not verifiable: two such builds at one revision share an\n+// identity while their code differs (review round 2, N4).\n+func engineIdentityFrom(info *debug.BuildInfo, ok bool) (string, bool) {\n \tidentity := version.Version\n-\tinfo, ok := debug.ReadBuildInfo()\n-\tif !ok {\n-\t\treturn identity\n+\tif !ok || info == nil {\n+\t\treturn identity, true\n \t}\n \trevision, modified := \"\", false\n \tfor _, setting := range info.Settings {\n@@ -187,5 +197,5 @@ func validationEngineIdentity() string {\n \t\t\tidentity += \".dirty\"\n \t\t}\n \t}\n-\treturn identity\n+\treturn identity, !modified\n }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go\nindex 84ac96e5..2ecd33b0 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go\n@@ -369,3 +369,53 @@ func TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d b=%+v, want b re-run and failing\", code, checkOf(run, \"b\"))\n \t}\n }\n+\n+// A rename names its source module too (review round 2, N1).\n+func TestCarrySelectsBothSidesOfARename(t *testing.T) {\n+\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n+\tf.write(\"a/data.txt\", \"data\\n\")\n+\tf.commit(\"a data\")\n+\tf.baseline()\n+\tf.git(\"mv\", \"a/data.txt\", \"b/data.txt\")\n+\tf.commit(\"move data from a to b\")\n+\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n+\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a,b\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a rename executed %q, want both its source and destination modules\", got)\n+\t}\n+\tif !strings.Contains(run.Carry.Selected[\"a\"], \"a/data.txt\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"source module reason = %q\", run.Carry.Selected[\"a\"])\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// Names Git would quote keep their owner (review round 2, N3).\n+func TestCarryKeepsTheOwnerOfAQuotedName(t *testing.T) {\n+\tf := newCarryFixture(t, `{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"modules\":{\"a\":{\"selfContained\":true},\"b\":{\"selfContained\":true}}}`)\n+\tf.baseline()\n+\tf.write(\"a/naïve \\\"name\\\".txt\", \"x\\n\")\n+\tf.commit(\"quoted name in a\")\n+\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n+\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a quoted name in a executed %q, want a\", got)\n+\t}\n+\tif !strings.HasPrefix(run.Carry.Selected[\"a\"], \"contains changed file: a/\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"reason = %q, want ownership by a\", run.Carry.Selected[\"a\"])\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+// A root module owns root files even when it declares itself self-contained\n+// (review round 2, N2).\n+func TestCarryRootModuleOwnsRootFiles(t *testing.T) {\n+\tf := newCarryFixture(t, `{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"modules\":{\".\":{\"selfContained\":true},\"a\":{\"selfContained\":true},\"b\":{\"selfContained\":true}}}`)\n+\tf.write(\"go.mod\", \"module root\\n\")\n+\tf.commit(\"root module\")\n+\tf.baseline()\n+\tf.write(\"main.go\", \"package main\\n\")\n+\tf.commit(\"root change\")\n+\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n+\tif _, ok := run.Carry.Selected[\".\"]; !ok {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"selected = %v, want the root module selected by its own file\", run.Carry.Selected)\n+\t}\n+\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); strings.Contains(got, \"a\") || strings.Contains(got, \"b\") || got == \"\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed %q, want only the root module\", got)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..6d0a5bf0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@\n+package cli\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"runtime/debug\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/version\"\n+)\n+\n+// A modified build is not a comparable engine identity (spec\n+// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks, review round 2, N4).\n+func TestEngineIdentityRefusesModifiedBuilds(t *testing.T) {\n+\tinfo := func(settings ...debug.BuildSetting) *debug.BuildInfo { return &debug.BuildInfo{Settings: settings} }\n+\tcases := []struct {\n+\t\tname       string\n+\t\tinfo       *debug.BuildInfo\n+\t\twant       string\n+\t\tverifiable bool\n+\t}{\n+\t\t{\"clean\", info(debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.revision\", Value: \"abc\"}, debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.modified\", Value: \"false\"}), version.Version + \"+abc\", true},\n+\t\t{\"modified\", info(debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.revision\", Value: \"abc\"}, debug.BuildSetting{Key: \"vcs.modified\", Value: \"true\"}), version.Version + \"+abc.dirty\", false},\n+\t\t{\"unstamped\", info(), version.Version, true},\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, tc := range cases {\n+\t\tgot, verifiable := engineIdentityFrom(tc.info, true)\n+\t\tif got != tc.want || verifiable != tc.verifiable {\n+\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%s: identity %q verifiable %v, want %q %v\", tc.name, got, verifiable, tc.want, tc.verifiable)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\nindex 05e582c2..f4cdd341 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n@@ -71,7 +71,10 @@ func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule, ex\n \t\t\treturn sel, fmt.Errorf(\"unsafe git revision: %q\", rev)\n \t\t}\n \t}\n-\tgitArgs := []string{\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", base}\n+\t// NUL-delimited and without rename pairing: a quoted or multi-line name\n+\t// keeps its owner, and a rename names both its source and its destination\n+\t// (review round 2, N1 and N3).\n+\tgitArgs := []string{\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", \"--no-renames\", base}\n \tif head != \"\" {\n \t\tgitArgs = append(gitArgs, head)\n \t}\n@@ -80,18 +83,18 @@ func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule, ex\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\treturn sel, fmt.Errorf(\"git diff %s failed (revision unknown?)\", sel.rangeLabel())\n \t}\n-\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n-\t\tif line != \"\" {\n-\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(line))\n+\tfor _, name := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n+\t\tif name != \"\" {\n+\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(name))\n \t\t}\n \t}\n \tif head == \"\" {\n \t\t// Worktree comparisons must also see untracked files — invisible new\n \t\t// code must never silently narrow validation (safe execution wins).\n-\t\tif untracked, err := posemodel.RawHistoryGit(root, \"ls-files\", \"--others\", \"--exclude-standard\").Output(); err == nil {\n-\t\t\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(untracked)), \"\\n\") {\n-\t\t\t\tif line != \"\" {\n-\t\t\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(line))\n+\t\tif untracked, err := posemodel.RawHistoryGit(root, \"ls-files\", \"-z\", \"--others\", \"--exclude-standard\").Output(); err == nil {\n+\t\t\tfor _, name := range strings.Split(string(untracked), \"\\x00\") {\n+\t\t\t\tif name != \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(name))\n \t\t\t\t}\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n@@ -130,9 +133,11 @@ func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule, ex\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t} else {\n \t\t\t// Root-level or unmapped change: safe execution wins, except for\n-\t\t\t// modules that declare their checks read nothing outside them.\n+\t\t\t// modules that declare their checks read nothing outside them. A\n+\t\t\t// root module owns the files no other module owns, so its\n+\t\t\t// declaration never exempts it from them (review round 2, N2).\n \t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n-\t\t\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained {\n+\t\t\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained && m.Rel != \".\" {\n \t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t\t}\n \t\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat .pose/workflows/review.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat .pose/workflows/review.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-22\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: pose-changed-scope-validation, pose-validation-evidence-material-binding\nremediates:\npriority: 1\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers:\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet `pose validate` refresh the canonical structured result by running only the checks a change can affect and carrying forward, unchanged and labelled, the results of the checks it cannot affect.\n\n### Business value\nIn the Harne8 batch of 2026-11-22, every material change to one module required the whole 72-check matrix again. Each run took about 35 minutes with three jobs. Intermittent failures in unrelated suites made roughly one run in three useless. A hooks change confined to `scripts/` needed four full runs in one review round. The canonical record is all-or-nothing today. `--changed-from` already selects the affected modules, but it writes a partial record that cannot replace the canonical one in `results_path`, so closeout still needs the full run.\n\n### Constraints\nA carried result must say where it ran. It never upgrades its outcome, so a carried failure stays a failure. Any doubt about eligibility widens to a full run, and the record states why. The selection reuses the changed-scope rules and widens them only by explicit declaration. Results and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n### Non-goals\nCaching individual command outputs, hashing arbitrary inputs of a check, carrying across a matrix or engine change, and changing how review bundles seal evidence beyond reading the per-check head.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: `pose validate --carry --json-out <results_path>` shall read the canonical record at the configured `results_path` as its base. It shall be refused with exit 2 when no `results_path` is configured, when the output path is not that path, or when combined with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n- R2: The base shall be used only when every condition below holds. Otherwise the run shall execute every check and record `carry.used: false` with the first failing condition as `carry.reason`.\n  - Its schema, mode, `matrix_sha256` and `engine_version` equal the current run's. Neither engine build is modified from its recorded revision.\n  - Its `git_head` is an ancestor of, or equal to, `HEAD`.\n  - It was not cancelled.\n  - It recorded `material_clean`: its own working copy matched its head when it started and when it ended, and none of its material bindings is unavailable.\n  - The working copy is clean in material paths.\n- R3: With a usable base, the run shall select modules with the changed-scope rules over `base.git_head..HEAD`, excluding the `results_path` file itself. It shall also exclude derived administrative records of the engine's closed list (`posemodel.IsAdministrativePath`, the classes material bindings ignore) that are regular files, or absent, at `HEAD`. Otherwise the index and state commits that follow every record would select every module. It shall execute every check of a selected module. Every check of an unselected module whose base result exists and was not skipped shall be carried. A module with any check lacking such a base result shall be selected with the reason `carry: no prior result for <check id>`.\n- R4: A carried check shall keep the base result's outcome, exit code, duration, limit state, output and evidence class. It shall add `carried_from`, the head the check actually ran at, preserved through chained carries, and `carried_generated_at`. Counts shall add `carried`. Carried failures and errors shall count toward the run outcome exactly like executed ones.\n- R5: The record shall add `engine_version` on every run: the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from, when the build records it. With `--carry` it shall also add a `carry` object: `used`, `reason`, `base_head`, `changed` (the number of files) and `selected` (module to reason).\n- R6: The changed set shall read Git names NUL-delimited and without rename pairing, so a quoted or multi-line name keeps its owner and a rename names both its source and its destination. A root module `.` owns the files no other module owns, whatever it declares. A module declared `\"selfContained\": true` in `.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json` shall not be selected by a change outside every module. It is still selected by changes inside it, by its declared `dependsOn` and by policy. This shall hold for `--changed-from` and `--carry` alike, with the reason recorded for the modules it widens.\n- R7: Reading the record into the delivery index shall use a check's `carried_from` and `carried_generated_at` as that result's head and time. Every consumer of a result's head therefore judges the commit a carried check really ran at.\n\n### Non-functional\n- R8: A run where every module is carried shall execute no check and finish in the time of the selection and the record write.\n\n### Security\n- R9: Revisions read from the base record shall pass the same confinement as `--changed-from` revisions before any Git call.\n\n### Compatibility\n- R10: A base record without `engine_version` shall never be used. Records without the new fields shall be read exactly as before.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp/internal/cli` (argument parsing, the run loop and record fields of `validate`, changed-scope selection), `pose-mcp/internal/pose` (reading the structured result into delivery results), the manual and the CLI help.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\n- created: .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_engine_identity_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n### API/contract changes\nAdditive fields in the structured validation result: `engine_version`, `carry`, `counts.carried`, and per check `carried_from` and `carried_generated_at`. There is a new `validate --carry` flag and a new optional `selfContained` field in module metadata.\n\n### Data/storage changes\nNone beyond the additive record fields.\n\n### Technical risks\nAn instance that marks a module `selfContained` while its checks read root files would carry stale results. The declaration is explicit, documented and opt-in, and the default stays the full fallback.\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [x] ADR for carrying results and the self-contained declaration.\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Record fields and base eligibility.\n- [x] Selection excluding the results file, the self-contained declaration and the no-prior-result widening.\n- [x] Carried entries in the run loop and counts.\n- [x] Per-check head in the delivery reading.\n- [x] Manual and help.\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Unit and fixture tests for every requirement, each shown failing before its code.\n- [x] `pose validate --module pose-mcp` and `check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\nA carried result is the base's result, not a new observation. It keeps the head where it ran, and consumers judge that head. The run's material binding is still computed at `HEAD`. This holds only because the selection guarantees that a carried module's inputs did not change between the two heads, under the changed-scope rules and the instance's declarations. See ADR `2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`.\n\n### Decision D2\nEligibility compares the whole matrix digest and the engine version instead of per-check definitions. A matrix or engine change reruns everything. That is the existing rule for material bindings, and it keeps the comparison obviously sound.\n\n### Decision D3\nOut-of-module changes keep selecting every module unless a module declares itself self-contained. Instances opt in per module, so a repository that never declares anything gets today's behaviour.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nGo tests in `internal/cli` drive real Git fixtures through `validate --carry`. They cover:\n- the base conditions of R2, one fixture each;\n- a change in one module that carries the other;\n- chained carries;\n- a carried failure;\n- the self-contained declaration of R6, under both flags;\n- the refusals of R1;\n- the per-check head reading of R7.\n\nEach test is run against the code before its requirement and must fail there.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/cli/ ./internal/pose/` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --module pose-mcp`.\n\n#### Lint\n- Command: `go vet ./...` (in `pose-mcp`).\n\n#### Typecheck\n- Command: compiled by the tests.\n\n#### Build\n- Command: `go build ./...` (in `pose-mcp`).\n\n#### Security / Contract\n- Command: `pose check --strict`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-22: the new fixtures in `validate_carry_test.go` and `delivery_validation_run_test.go` drive real Git repositories through `validate --carry`. Each requirement's code was mutated out and its test failed: the per-check head reading, the self-contained declaration, the canonical-result exclusion and the derived-record exclusion.\n- 2026-11-22: `go test ./...` in `pose-mcp` first failed on `TestDirectPrintSitesOnlyShrink` and `TestEmbeddedDistMatchesPoseDist`; the carry lines now go through `cliout` and the embedded manual was regenerated. A full `pose validate` then failed the post-install gate because the manual cited derived-record directories absent from a fresh install; the text was rewritten without those paths.\n- 2026-11-22: `pose validate --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json` with a binary built from `606a0fa8` passed 236 of 236 checks in 786 s.\n- 2026-11-22: dogfood: `validate --carry --explain` at `c4182727` used that record as base. The record and index commits counted as zero changed files. Five of six modules were carried, and `pose-mcp` ran because its criticality is high. The run passed with `counts.carried` 3, and the record is kept at `.pose/bin/batch-20261118/engine-a-dogfood-carried.json` in the Harne8 batch.\n\n- 2026-11-22: review round 1 (rrn-0dd98c4aab8b5918, Codex) found F1 (high). A base that validated a dirty working copy could be carried. The record now has `material_clean`, and a base without it, or with an unavailable material binding, is refused. `TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy` reproduces the sequence. It failed with the check mutated out and passes with it.\n- 2026-11-22: review round 2 (rrn-1c033e4c349d7794) confirmed R1-F1 fixed and found four new issues. N1: a rename's source path was missing from the changed set. N2: a self-contained root module was exempt from its own files. N3: quoted, newline-delimited Git names lost their owner. N4: modified engine builds at one revision shared an identity. All four are fixed, each with a regression test that failed when its fix was mutated out.\n\n### Results summary\nAll new and existing tests pass, and the strict matrix is green. In this repository, `pose-mcp` holds 233 of the 236 checks and is high criticality, so carrying saves little here. In an instance with many modules, a change confined to one of them reruns only that module and the modules that are not declared self-contained.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRefusesIncompatibleFlags\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun test:TestCarryRefusesAFilteredBase test:TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy test:TestEngineIdentityRefusesModifiedBuilds\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule test:TestCarryRecordFileIsNotAChange test:TestCarryIgnoresDerivedAdministrativeRecords test:TestCarrySelectsAModuleWithoutAPriorResult\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries test:TestCarryKeepsACarriedFailureAFailure\n- R5 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule\n- R6 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRootChangeRunsEverythingUnlessSelfContained test:TestChangedScopeHonoursSelfContainedModules test:TestCarrySelectsBothSidesOfARename test:TestCarryKeepsTheOwnerOfAQuotedName test:TestCarryRootModuleOwnsRootFiles\n- R7 [satisfied] test:TestDeliveryValidationRunReadsTheCarriedHead\n- R8 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRecordFileIsNotAChange\n- R9 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun\n- R10 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun\n\nR9 is enforced by `safeGitRevRE` in `planCarry` before any Git call; the not-an-ancestor case drives a forged head through it. R10 is the `base without engine version` case.\n\n### Known gaps\nReview bundles and closeout consume the per-check head through the delivery index. No separate end-to-end test seals a bundle over a carried record.\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n`pose validate --carry` refreshes the canonical structured result. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select since the canonical base and carries the other checks with their outcome and the head where they ran. The changed set ignores the result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings already ignore. Any doubt about the base runs everything, and the record says why. Modules can declare `selfContained` to stop being widened by changes outside every module. The delivery index reads a carried result as an observation of its own head. The record now names the engine version together with its build revision.\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp/internal/cli` (validate, its scope selection, its result fields and the help catalogue), `pose-mcp/internal/pose` (the delivery reading of the result), the manual in English and pt-BR with their embedded copies, this spec and its ADR.\n\n### Validation executed\nSee the execution log: the package tests with mutation checks, the full `go test ./...` and the strict matrix with 236 of 236 checks.\n\n### Residual risks\nAn instance that declares a module self-contained while its checks read files outside it would carry a stale result. The declaration is explicit and documented, and the default stays the full fallback.\n\n### Follow-ups\n- [open] F1: cover with an end-to-end test that a review bundle sealed over a carried record judges the carried head (seal, verify and closeout-check on a fixture). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-15)\n# ADR: Carry unchanged checks into the canonical validation result\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted on 2026-11-22 for `pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`.\n\n## Context\n\nThe canonical structured result at `results_path` is the only validation evidence that review bundles and closeout load. Producing it means running the whole matrix. `--changed-from` already selects the modules a change can affect: it uses changed paths, declared `dependsOn` edges and the high-criticality policy, and falls back to every module for a change outside all modules. Its record, though, marks the other checks as skipped, so it cannot stand in for the canonical result. In an instance with a slow and partly intermittent matrix, every review round pays for the full run.\n\n## Decision\n\nAdd `validate --carry`. The flag uses the existing canonical result as a base when that base is provably comparable:\n- the same schema, mode, matrix digest and engine version;\n- a head that is an ancestor of the current head;\n- not cancelled;\n- a clean material working copy.\n\nWith a usable base, the run executes the changed-scope selection between the two heads. That selection ignores the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings already ignore: the engine's closed list, still regular files at `HEAD`. It copies the base results of every unselected module. Each copied result keeps its outcome and states the head where it really ran (`carried_from`) and when (`carried_generated_at`). The delivery reading uses that head for the result. Any unmet condition runs everything and records the reason.\n\nA module may declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata. A change outside every module then stops selecting it. The declaration is the instance's statement that the module's checks read only its own directory and its declared dependencies, so it applies to `--changed-from` too. Without it, the existing safe fallback stays.\n\n## Consequences\n\nA change confined to one module reruns that module, its declared dependents and the modules that cannot claim self-containment. Everything else is reused with its true origin visible. Consumers that compare a result's head with a subject head now see carried results as observations of the earlier head, which is accurate. An incorrect `selfContained` declaration can carry a stale result. That risk sits with the instance's explicit declaration and is documented next to it.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Hashing each check's declared inputs would be more precise. It needs every check's inputs declared and verified, and it is a larger contract.\n- Merging a partial `--changed-from` record into the canonical file by hand would hide which results were reused.\n- Carrying across matrix or engine changes would make a definition change invisible to the evidence.\n# Workflow: Review\n\n## Objective\n\nVerify that a change is correct, production-safe, and aligned with its approved scope and specs.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- Make the final diff readable through cohesive commits or batches.\n- Provide requirement and spec context.\n- Attach implementer validation evidence, including `pose validate` output.\n- Define acceptance criteria and expected risk.\n\n## Execution checklist\n\n1. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` (or `pose_review_bundle`) when bundle policy is enabled; otherwise resolve `pose review-plan <scope> --explain`.\n2. Confirm the semantic scope, patch/tree identity, mapped components and bundle plan digest.\n3. Run required active tools first, evaluate recommended tools explicitly and leave completion-only tools deferred until an attestation exists.\n4. Select the plan's applicable rules explicitly and record them in the review.\n5. Resolve rule conflicts with the most restrictive option, prioritizing security for exposure, authorization, and integrity risks.\n6. Consult `.pose/knowledge/` for relevant handoffs, accepted risks, follow-ups, and decision logs.\n7. Check compliance with specs, contracts, and local instructions.\n8. Review functional correctness and edge cases.\n9. Evaluate security, observability, and performance risks.\n10. Require validation evidence from `.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json` proportional to risk.\n11. Identify regression and rollout or rollback risks.\n12. Run plan-selected assessments, including tech-debt and integration when applicable.\n13. Classify findings by severity and propose objective actions.\n14. **Produce a handoff** in `.pose/knowledge/` when findings result in accepted risk, post-merge monitoring, or deferred work (`pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>`); link it from the review.\n15. Seal the validated subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`; stop if any input is unclassified or changed since preparation.\n16. Issue a final decision: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n17. Record the decision with `pose review attest <bundle-id> ... --plan-digest <sha256> --apply`; signed external envelopes use `pose review attest --envelope <path> --apply`.\n18. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; reject stale, incomplete, conflicting or transitively invalid reused criteria.\n19. Apply closeout only after verification reports `ready-to-close`; lifecycle, state and report refreshes must not change the sealed bundle.\n20. When Contributor Mode is active and review identifies linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or POSE rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal via `pose contribute stage --type enhancement`.\n\n## Required rule selection\n\nAttach this section to the review:\n\n```md\n## Rules applied during review\n- Change type: <feature|bugfix|refactor|documentation-update|mixed>\n- Workflow consulted: `.pose/workflows/<file>.md`\n- Rules selected:\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/security.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md` when knowledge or process changes\n- Rationale for each selected rule: <one line per item>\n- Rules not applicable: <list and explain>\n```\n\n## Recurrence escalation\n\n- Trigger `.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md` when recurring rework is uncovered and the threshold is met.\n- Require `recurrence_rework_uncovered` evidence for the 30-day period.\n- Require explicit links from the specialized workflow to applicable domain rules.\n- Require a formal keep, adjust, or discard decision after the 45-day pilot.\n\n## Minimum domain coverage\n\n- React UI: apply `frontend-react`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Go services: apply `backend-go`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Cluster deployment or infrastructure: apply `kubernetes`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Process, spec, workflow, rule, or report: apply `documentation-style`, `knowledge-governance`, and security when sensitive data is involved.\n- Cross-stack changes: apply every touched domain rule cumulatively.\n\n## Domain review checklist\n\n### Security\n\n- Confirm authentication, authorization, and least privilege when applicable.\n- Verify that code, configuration, manifests, docs, and logs contain no secrets.\n- Require applicable vulnerability and secret-scanning evidence.\n\n### Contracts\n\n- Confirm compatibility of public HTTP, event, schema, CLI, and file contracts.\n- Validate forward and backward compatibility for rollout and rollback.\n- Require a spec update for contract changes.\n\n### Observability\n\n- Verify structured logs and metrics without sensitive data.\n- Confirm that probes, health checks, and alerts reflect real behavior.\n- Preserve enough traceability for post-deployment diagnosis.\n\n### Validation\n\n- Require lint, typecheck, test, and build coverage proportional to risk.\n- Require executed `pose validate` evidence and relevant results.\n- Record environment limitations and residual validation risks.\n\n## Editorial checklist\n\n- Use imperative, actionable instructions.\n- Keep bullets short and avoid duplicated sections.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- Reference explicit files and paths.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n- A review decision with rationale.\n- The inspected effective plan, its component provenance and tool dispositions.\n- An immutable sealed review bundle and separate attestation bound to its exact digest.\n- A completed rule-selection section.\n- Findings with severity, evidence, and recommendation.\n- Recurrence classification by domain and cause with preventive actions.\n- An explicit statement about public contracts and compatibility.\n- Residual risks and safe-deployment conditions.\n- References to executed checks and collected evidence.\n\n## Complete multi-rule review example\n\n```md\n## Review Summary\n- Decision: approved with reservations\n- Change type: feature (Go API, React UI, and Helm)\n- Workflow: `.pose/workflows/feature.md`\n\n## Rules applied during review\n- `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`): verified handlers, context, and error handling.\n- `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`): verified accessibility and explicit loading and error states.\n- `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`): verified resources, probes, and immutable images.\n- `.pose/rules/security.md`: verified authorization and absence of secrets.\n- `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`: verified editorial consistency.\n\n## Checks and evidence\n- `pose validate`: passed\n- `go test ./...`: passed in the backend module\n- `pnpm lint && pnpm test`: passed in the frontend module\n- `helm template` and `kubectl apply --dry-run=client`: passed\n\n## Contracts and compatibility\n- Preserved `POST /v1/storage`.\n- Added optional `retentionDays`, preserving backward compatibility.\n\n## Findings\n- Medium: missing queue-saturation alert; add a metric and alert before production.\n- Low: frontend error lacks request-id context; improve observable UX.\n\n## Residual risks\n- Review did not simulate real cluster load.\n- Increase monitoring during the first 24 hours.\n```\n\n## CI failure interpretation\n\n- Treat `POSE check (strict)` as merge-blocking on protected branches.\n- Treat failed required checks in `POSE validate (strict)` as merge-blocking.\n- Attach pipeline artifacts: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log`, and the POSE report.\n- Classify optional-check failures as quality reservations with an owned remediation plan.\n- Reject changes that violate specs, public contracts, security, or safe rollout.\n\n## Phased rollout for unready modules\n\n- Enforce the current required checks immediately.\n- Use `moduleOverrides` to phase adoption without weakening global structural and required gates.\n- Promote optional checks to required by module with an owner and agreed window.\n\n### Optional-to-required promotion protocol\n\n- Select a pilot domain and map candidate checks with owners and explicit risks.\n- Measure success for four weeks and require a baseline of at least 95 percent.\n- Promote only eligible domains through `moduleOverrides`.\n- Update the validation matrix and quality policy in the same change set.\n- Monitor regressions and adjust rollout without removing established required gates.\n- Update specs and rules when rollout changes merge acceptance criteria.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Resolve or formally accept every critical and high finding.\n- Make the final decision clear and actionable.\n- Support quality and security conclusions with evidence.\n- Preserve approved scope without unjustified drift.\n\n## Reviewer mode\n\n**Objective:** evaluate technical quality and production readiness with emphasis on correctness, risk, and scope.\n\n- **Focus:** functional correctness, spec consistency, regression and security risk, operability, validation quality, and actionable feedback.\n- **Anti-patterns:** approve without sufficient evidence; review style while ignoring functional risk; request unrelated changes; block progress on subjective preference.\n- **Handoff:** state the decision, severity and evidence for each finding, safe merge or deployment conditions, accepted risks, and recommended monitoring.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
